AssertionRecord Specification {#chapter-assertionrecord}
=======================================

## Definition ## {#section-assertionrecord-definition}

An <dfn>AssertionRecord</dfn> is a [=Record=] whose Record type is the Root AssertionRecord Schema or a subclass of it ([[#section-schemarecord-subclass]]). It preserves an attributable assertion that a subject stands in a relationship, identified by a predicate, to an object.

An AssertionRecord MUST NOT be interpreted as modifying the subject or the object. It preserves only the assertion made by its asserting Agent. An AssertionRecord MAY refer to Records in the same or in another namespace. A reference MUST NOT imply acceptance, storage, reciprocity, modification or any other obligation for the custodian of the referenced Record.

The content of a Version of an AssertionRecord is a JSON object in the primary representation (format `application/json`):

<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Field<th>Required<th>Type<th>Description</thead>
<tr><td><code>subject</code><td>MUST<td>DID or IRI<td>Resource from which the relationship is asserted
<tr><td><code>predicate</code><td>MUST<td>IRI<td>Relationship asserted (see [[#section-assertionrecord-predicates]])
<tr><td><code>object</code><td>MUST<td>DID or IRI<td>Resource to which the relationship is asserted
<tr><td><code>assertedBy</code><td>MUST<td>DID<td>Agent responsible for the assertion
<tr><td><code>assertedAt</code><td>MUST<td>ISO 8601<td>Time of the assertion
<tr><td><code>effectiveAt</code><td>MAY<td>ISO 8601<td>Time from which the relationship holds. MAY precede <code>assertedAt</code>. A predicate declaration MAY make it required
</table>

<pre class="example json" title="AssertionRecord content">
{
  "subject": "did:rwp:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;recordA&gt;:&lt;versionHash&gt;",
  "predicate": "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom",
  "object": "did:rwp:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;recordB&gt;",
  "assertedBy": "did:rwp:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;agentId&gt;",
  "assertedAt": "2026-08-13T11:00:00Z",
  "effectiveAt": "2026-08-13T11:00:00Z"
}
</pre>


## Taxonomy ## {#section-assertionrecord-taxonomy}

<pre class="highlight language-text">
@prefix rw: &lt;https://w3id.org/recordweb/ontology#&gt; .
rw:AssertionRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:RelationRecord .
</pre>

## Subject and Object ## {#section-assertionrecord-resources}

`subject` and `object` are each the DID of an RWP Record, another DID, or an absolute IRI ([[RFC3987]]), subject to the constraints of the applicable predicate declaration.

A DID with a `versionHash` refers to exactly that Version. Where the Version is resolvable, a validating implementation MUST verify that it belongs to the identified Record. A DID without `versionHash` refers to the Record independent of a specific Version.

The meaning of the subject and the object MUST be interpretable according to the applicable predicate and SchemaRecord.


## Predicates ## {#section-assertionrecord-predicates}

The value of `predicate` MUST be an absolute IRI ([[RFC3987]]). The meaning of an asserted relationship MUST NOT be carried solely by natural-language text. A human-readable label MUST NOT replace the predicate IRI and MUST NOT be the sole basis for semantic interpretation or validation.

The Root AssertionRecord Schema permits any absolute IRI as `predicate`. A SchemaRecord that is a subclass of it MAY restrict the permitted predicates and the permitted kinds of `subject` and `object` in its `jsonSchema`.


### Core predicates ### {#subsection-assertionrecord-core-predicates}

The following PROV-O predicates are the core relationship vocabulary of RWP:

<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Predicate<th>Subject<th>Object</thead>
<tr><td><code>prov:wasAttributedTo</code><td>Entity<td>Agent
<tr><td><code>prov:wasGeneratedBy</code><td>Entity<td>Activity
<tr><td><code>prov:wasDerivedFrom</code><td>Entity<td>Entity
<tr><td><code>prov:wasRevisionOf</code><td>Entity<td>Entity
<tr><td><code>prov:actedOnBehalfOf</code><td>Agent<td>Agent
</table>

A Record is an Entity for the purposes of this section. A Record MUST NOT be the subject or object of a PROV-O predicate whose declared semantics require an Agent or an Activity. An AssertionRecord using a PROV-O predicate MUST satisfy the domain and range of that predicate.

For provenance and version-lineage relationships, an AssertionRecord MUST use an applicable PROV-O predicate where PROV-O expresses the intended semantics. A SchemaRecord MUST NOT permit a predicate whose semantics duplicate an applicable PROV-O predicate.


### Other predicates ### {#subsection-assertionrecord-other-predicates}

Institutional, functional, legal, subject-matter and archival-context relationships are not necessarily provenance relationships. They MUST NOT be represented with a PROV-O predicate unless that predicate expresses the intended semantics.

A predicate that is not a core predicate MUST have a <dfn>predicate declaration</dfn>. The declaration MUST be versioned and resolvable through the predicate IRI, and MUST provide

- a human-readable definition of the semantics,
- the relationship direction,
- the permitted subject and object kinds,
- the required or permitted assertion data,
- the validation requirements, and
- for an externally defined predicate, the vocabulary and its version.

The declaration SHOULD provide machine-readable representations where available, for example RDF, JSON-LD, SHACL or JSON Schema. A localised display label MAY accompany the declaration. It MUST NOT replace the predicate IRI as the semantic identifier.

A SchemaRecord MAY constrain a predicate more narrowly than its declaration. It MUST NOT broaden, reverse or contradict the semantics or direction defined by the declaration. A conformance profile MAY require support for specified predicates. It MUST NOT define a predicate or alter the semantics of one.

INFO: RiC-O predicates are an example of contextual predicates. A SchemaRecord MAY permit them. Their use does not require an implementation to store, exchange or process Records as RDF or JSON-LD: the RiC-O IRI identifies the semantics of the asserted relationship. The declaration identifies the exact RiC-O predicate IRI and the RiC-O version to which it applies, and a SchemaRecord does not permit a RiC-O predicate where an applicable PROV-O predicate expresses the intended semantics.


## Relationship to the Version Graph ## {#section-assertionrecord-version-graph}

The version graph is the authoritative representation of version succession between Versions of the same Record. A conforming implementation MUST determine version ancestry, direct parentage, branches and merges exclusively from the `parents` field and the rules in [[#section-payload-version-graph]].

An AssertionRecord using `prov:wasRevisionOf`, `prov:wasDerivedFrom` or another provenance predicate MUST NOT create, alter, replace or repair a version-graph edge. Where it documents a relationship between Versions of the same Record, it MAY provide additional attributable evidence, but MUST NOT be used to infer a parent relationship that the version graph does not contain.

If an AssertionRecord and the version graph make incompatible statements about version succession, the version graph is authoritative for all versioning, ancestry, finalisation, merge and integrity decisions. The AssertionRecord remains an attributable assertion. The conflict MUST NOT alter the integrity or history of either Record.


## Validation ## {#section-assertionrecord-validation}

An AssertionRecord MUST be validated against its SchemaRecord and the type chain of that SchemaRecord before it is finalised. Validation MUST verify at least that

- `predicate` is an absolute IRI,
- `subject`, `predicate` and `object` are present,
- `subject` and `object` satisfy the constraints declared for the predicate,
- the asserting Agent and the time of assertion are present, and
- the AssertionRecord satisfies the normal Record integrity and finalisation requirements.

An AssertionRecord MAY be valid even where a referenced external resource cannot be resolved, provided that it identifies the resource unambiguously and the applicable SchemaRecord permits unresolved references. A failure to resolve a referenced resource MUST NOT be interpreted as a modification of, or rejection by, the custodian of that resource.

INFO: Where the target namespace exposes an LDN inbox, the asserting party MAY notify the target custodian that a new AssertionRecord refers to one of its Records. Such a notification is informational only. It MUST NOT create an obligation to store, mirror, accept or reciprocate the relationship.

